      "text": "The nucleus is the small, dense center of an atom. It contains protons and neutrons.\n\nIn a diagram, you can identify the nucleus by looking for the center of the atom.\n\nHow do you identify the nucleus?\n\nProtons (+): Positively charged particles inside the nucleus.\n\nNeutrons (0): Neutral particles inside the nucleus.\n\nElectrons (−): Negatively charged particles found in the region surrounding the nucleus.\n\nHow does this relate to covalent radius?\n\nWhen measuring covalent radius, scientists measure the distance between the nuclei of two bonded atoms, then divide by 2 if the atoms are identical.\n\nFor example, if two chlorine atoms are bonded:\n\nC\nl\n−\nC\nl\nCl−Cl\n\nAnd the distance between their nuclei is 198 pm:\n\nCovalent radius\n=\n198\n2\n=\n99\n pm\nCovalent radius=\n2\n198\n\t​\n\n=99 pm\n\nImportant: The nucleus is not something scientists identify by seeing a little dot inside the atom. Its position can be determined using experimental techniques, including diffraction and spectroscopy.\n\nWould you like me to explain how to find the number of protons, neutrons, and electrons using the periodic table?",
