      "text": "Scientists measure covalent radius indirectly. They don't use a tiny ruler to measure an atom! Instead, they determine the distance between the nuclei of two bonded atoms using experimental techniques.\n\n1. How scientists actually measure it\n\nOne common technique is X-ray crystallography.\n\nnature.com\n\nHere's how it works:\n\nShine X-rays at a crystal. The crystal contains atoms arranged in a repeating pattern.\n\nX-rays scatter off the electrons. This creates a pattern of spots called a diffraction pattern.\n\nAnalyze the pattern. Scientists use mathematical calculations to reconstruct the electron density and determine atomic positions.\n\nCalculate the bond length. They find the distance between the centers of two bonded atoms, which closely correspond to the positions of their nuclei.\n\nDivide by 2 if the bonded atoms are identical.\n\n2. Example: Chlorine (Cl₂)\n\nSimplified illustration of the distance between chlorine nuclei\n\nScientists can determine that the distance between the two chlorine nuclei is approximately 198 picometers.\n\nThen:\n\nCovalent radius\n=\n198\n2\nCovalent radius=\n2\n198\n\t​\n\n99\n pm\n99 pm\n\t​\n\n3. But how do they measure molecules that aren't crystals?\n\nScientists can also use microwave spectroscopy and electron diffraction to determine bond lengths in gas-phase molecules.\n\nFor example, microwave spectroscopy measures molecular rotation. From the rotational energy levels, scientists can calculate the molecule's moment of inertia and then its bond length.\n\nThe important thing to remember\n\nScientists generally don't observe nuclei directly when measuring covalent radius. They use the way X-rays, electrons, or electromagnetic radiation interact with matter to calculate the distance between atomic nuclei.\n\nOne subtle detail: Covalent radius isn't always exactly the same for an element. It can vary depending on whether the atoms form single, double, or triple bonds.\n\nWould you like to understand how X-ray diffraction patterns reveal the positions of atoms? That's the interesting part of how the measurement works.",
