    "text": "Covalent Radius — Explained Simply\n\nCovalent radius is the distance from the nucleus of an atom to the midpoint of a covalent bond it forms with an identical atom.\n\nIn simpler words, it's a way of measuring how big an atom is when it's bonded to another atom.\n\n1. How do we measure covalent radius?\n\nImagine two hydrogen atoms sharing electrons to form an H₂ molecule.\n\nSimplified illustration of the H–H bond. Blue dots represent nuclei.\n\nThe distance between the two hydrogen nuclei is approximately 74 picometers (pm).\n\nSince the atoms are identical, divide the distance by 2.\n\nCovalent radius\n=\nBond length\n2\nCovalent radius=\n2\nBond length\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n74\n2\n=\n37\n pm\n2\n74\n\t​\n\n=\n37 pm\n\t​\n\n\nSo hydrogen has a covalent radius of approximately 37 pm.\n\n2. Covalent radius trends on the periodic table\n\nThis is the most important part to understand for chemistry tests.\n\nSelected main-group elements. Covalent radius generally increases down a group and decreases from left to right across a period.\nAcross a period (left → right)\n\nCovalent radius decreases.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe number of protons increases.\n\nElectrons are added to the same main energy level.\n\nThe nucleus pulls the electrons more strongly.\n\nThe atoms become smaller.\n\nExample:\n\nLi\n>\nBe\n>\nB\n>\nC\n>\nN\nLi>Be>B>C>N\nDown a group (top → bottom)\n\nCovalent radius increases.\n\nWhy?\n\nAtoms gain additional electron shells.\n\nThe outer electrons are farther from the nucleus.\n\nInner electrons shield outer electrons from nuclear attraction.\n\nThe atoms become larger.\n\nExample:\n\nF\n<\nCl\n<\nBr\n<\nI\nF<Cl<Br<I\n3. Easy trick to remember\n\nAtomic size generally increases toward the bottom-left of the periodic table.\n\nFor introductory chemistry, covalent radius follows the same general trend as atomic radius.\n\n4. Test your understanding!\n\nQUESTION 3 OF 3\n\n2 correct\n\nTwo identical atoms have a bond length of 160 pm. What is their covalent radius?\n\n160 pm\n80 pm\n320 pm\nPrevious\nTry again\n\nThe key takeaway: Covalent radius is half the bond length between two identical bonded atoms. It generally decreases across a period and increases down a group.",
